//
// P1383R2 ("More constexpr for <cmath> and <complex>") requires the transcendental complex
// functions below (sqrt, exp, log, pow, and the trig/hyperbolic families) to be usable in a
// constant expression. The compiler this is built against has no constant-evaluation support for
// any transcendental math builtin (__builtin_sqrt, __builtin_exp, __builtin_sin, etc. all fail to
// constant-evaluate -- checked directly against clang/lib/AST/ByteCode/InterpBuiltin.cpp, which has
// zero case labels for any of them, and confirmed empirically against both this fork and a current
// stock Clang release, so this isn't a fork-specific gap to backport). The functions below are
// hand-rolled, real-valued replacements usable via `if consteval`, deliberately kept private to
// this header rather than added to the shared __math:: primitives: __math::sqrt and friends must
// keep meaning "call the platform's sqrt", used by every scalar <cmath> caller, and are out of
// scope here -- this is <complex>'s own accuracy problem to solve, not <cmath>'s.
//
// These do not aim for bit-identical results against the runtime path (typical libm transcendental
// functions aren't correctly rounded either, and the standard grants <cmath> implementation-defined
// accuracy latitude generally); they aim for full working precision via textbook range-reduction
// techniques, verified against an independent oracle. sqrt is the one function here where IEEE 754
// mandates correct rounding for the runtime path, and Newton-Raphson from a decent starting point
// converges to within it.

